{"title":"Sharma Math Exam 2026 Problems/Problem 5","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1_(Dynamic_Programming)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1 (Dynamic Programming)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(Linearity_of_Expectation_&amp;_Bash)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (Linearity of Expectation &amp; Bash)</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#First_guess\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">First guess</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Second_guess\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Second guess</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Third_guess\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Third guess</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Fourth_guess\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Fourth guess</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Fifth_guess\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Fifth guess</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Sixth_guess\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Sixth guess</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Sharma_Math_Exam_2026_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Nathan is playing a game where he has to guess whether a card is blue or red. He starts off with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> cards, and he knows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> of them are blue and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> of them are red. Nathan always plays optimally. An example scenario of the game for the first three turns is shown below. The total expected number of cards he will correctly guess by the end of the game can be expressed as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/f/27f583697295fc6f723862c13228ee55107c33ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{m}{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"22\" height=\"34\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> are relatively prime positive integers. Evaluate <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/b/53bf1afcb274a3bb84338109fffec8a1f1c02008.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m + n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"48\" height=\"12\" />. \n</p><p><b>Example scenario for first three turns:</b>\n</p>\n<ul><li>Nathan guesses blue on the first turn</li>\n<li>His first turn guess turns out to be correct</li>\n<li>Nathan guesses blue on the second turn</li>\n<li>His second turn guess turns out to be correct</li>\n<li>Nathan will choose to guess red on the third turn</li></ul>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/7/ba7474e70a5eea8a4c5730f9317c1b26ac0677b9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) } 204 \\qquad\\textbf{(B) } 239 \\qquad\\textbf{(C) } 169 \\qquad\\textbf{(D) } 134 \\qquad\\textbf{(E) } 180$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"446\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_1_.28Dynamic_Programming.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1_(Dynamic_Programming)\">Solution 1 (Dynamic Programming)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Sharma_Math_Exam_2026_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1 (Dynamic Programming)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/a/faaa56fd2cef98b218306381a25d769cd851c131.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E(B,R)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"67\" height=\"20\" /> be a function representing the maximum expected number of cards to be guessed with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> blue cards and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> red cards remaining. Since the player plays optimally, they will always guess the color they think most cards have. So the probability of a correct guess is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/2/b729f9b639a60674e667241009289c23de10325d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\max(B,R)}{B + R}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"91\" height=\"42\" />, and the probability of an incorrect guess is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/2/d82dc485a23c50ea377f53df1d0039901a240992.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\min(B,R)}{B + R}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"87\" height=\"42\" />. We have \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/a/5aa5f2043b45ce0fe8741262280843d7881b38bc.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[E(B,R) = \\frac{\\max(B,R)}{B + R}(1 + E(\\text{state after a correct guess})) + \\frac{\\min(B,R)}{B + R}(E(\\text{state after an incorrect guess}))\\]\" width=\"781\" height=\"40\" /> In <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/4/5f4591d8a12bf121d47a561677c0af29aa489ace.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E(\\text{state after a correct guess})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"218\" height=\"20\" />, the guessed card count decreases by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/6/a26edc993f927f92dc1e0d21f77decaf6a47a930.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E(\\text{state after an incorrect guess})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"241\" height=\"20\" />, the not guessed card count decreases by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. \n</p><p>Our answer is represented by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/4/45471e26735278aa42f4e6c6a947814379791f98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E(4,3)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"57\" height=\"20\" />, as this represents the expected number of guesses given <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> blue cards and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> red cards. To find this, we will first iteratively calculate <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/a/faaa56fd2cef98b218306381a25d769cd851c131.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E(B,R)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"67\" height=\"20\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/b/febe175e0eb53559754dce8f46ce9b4fc7a752a1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2,3,4,5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"60\" height=\"16\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> cards. Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/7/257d907dbe88f815a82b2c4e23781f4f04f202aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E(0,n) = E(n,0) = n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"175\" height=\"20\" />, since if we are only left with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> cards of one color, we are guaranteed to guess all of them correctly.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> cards: \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/5/295c735567c8f254836feaaf79825d76b35a9591.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[E(1,1) = \\frac{1}{2}(1 + E(0,1)) + \\frac{1}{2}E(1,0)= \\frac{1}{2}(1 + 1) + \\frac{1}{2}(1) = \\frac{3}{2}\\]\" width=\"468\" height=\"37\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> cards: \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/0/560d73531d75e6765a85660b7752c81606b53d93.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[E(2,1) = \\frac{2}{3} (1 + E(1,1)) + \\frac{1}{3} E(2,0) = \\frac{2}{3}(1 + \\frac{3}{2}) + \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{7}{3}\\]\" width=\"449\" height=\"38\" />  \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> cards: \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/3/183e2f1bafb6858b79c0b3bf3735179eaeb21069.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[E(3,1) = \\frac{3}{4} (1 + E(2,1)) + \\frac{1}{4}E(3,0) = \\frac{3}{4} (1 + \\frac{7}{3}) + \\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{13}{4}\\]\" width=\"458\" height=\"38\" /> \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/e/1ae103aa7718d0af3f91c5333fce19a5b40b3449.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[E(2,2) = \\frac{1}{2} (1 + E(2,1)) + \\frac{1}{2} E(1,2) = \\frac{1}{2} (1 + \\frac{7}{3}) + \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{7}{3} = \\frac{17}{6}\\]\" width=\"484\" height=\"38\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> cards: \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/2/4021b13c1bf690c2491ede86c240dcd7d478b569.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[E(3,2) = \\frac{3}{5} (1 + E(2,2)) + \\frac{2}{5} E(3,1) = \\frac{3}{5} (1 + \\frac{17}{6}) + \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot \\frac{13}{4} = \\frac{18}{5}\\]\" width=\"502\" height=\"38\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/e/a2efee8906426ee40da4ebb86a7b8a282499442a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[E(4,1) = \\frac{4}{5} (1 + E(3,1)) + \\frac{1}{5} E(4,0) = \\frac{4}{5} (1 + \\frac{13}{4}) + \\frac{1}{5}(4) = \\frac{21}{5}\\]\" width=\"490\" height=\"37\" />.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> cards:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/5/7f5142aeb17ab11502fae34f13f2e58ac2a8a7d0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[E(3,3) = \\frac{1}{2} (1 + E(2,3)) + \\frac{1}{2} E(3,2) = \\frac{1}{2} (1 + \\frac{18}{5}) + \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{18}{5} = \\frac{41}{10}\\]\" width=\"502\" height=\"37\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/a/a3aed3fe63fc271f729103f148118b8efc430728.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[E(4,2) = \\frac{2}{3} (1 + E(3,2)) + \\frac{1}{3} E(4,1) = \\frac{2}{3} (1 + \\frac{18}{5}) + \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\frac{21}{5} = \\frac{67}{15}\\]\" width=\"502\" height=\"38\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/f/e5fc761bd86337626d51f3660bd9e50cacfc2dd2.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[E(5,1) = \\frac{5}{6} (1 + E(4,1)) + \\frac{1}{6} E(5,0) = \\frac{5}{6} (1 + \\frac{21}{5}) + \\frac{1}{6}(5) = \\frac{31}{6}\\]\" width=\"490\" height=\"38\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> cards: \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/7/767c71e387074e4da52e17f69382aaea6433ea6b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[E(4,3) = \\frac{4}{7} (1 + E(3,3)) + \\frac{3}{7} E(4,2) = \\frac{4}{7} (1 + \\frac{41}{10}) + \\frac{3}{7} \\cdot \\frac{67}{15} = \\frac{169}{35}\\]\" width=\"511\" height=\"38\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/2/ea2ebdddbe25fd8bec9f9d5aee7302d9ccc8a54e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$169 + 35 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(A) } 204}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"168\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28Linearity_of_Expectation_.26_Bash.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(Linearity_of_Expectation_&amp;_Bash)\">Solution 2 (Linearity of Expectation &amp; Bash)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Sharma_Math_Exam_2026_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (Linearity of Expectation &amp; Bash)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Recall that Linearity of Expectation allows us to sum individual expected values to get the expected value of a desired event. \nWe will do casework on the expected number of cards Nathan will guess correctly for each guess and add them up to get our final answer.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"First_guess\">First guess</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Sharma_Math_Exam_2026_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: First guess\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>The most optimal move is for Nathan to guess blue, with an expected value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/b/07b007731c8aa702e6c3bdbb06ec8e415ab7dc92.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4/7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"27\" height=\"18\" /> since there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> blues and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> reds. \n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Second_guess\">Second guess</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Sharma_Math_Exam_2026_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Second guess\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>If Nathan correctly guessed a blue on his first move, he is left with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> reds and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> blues, with an expected value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/3/1f3cc0ad966b3e637cd199607cbf0d1befc0e491.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1/2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"29\" height=\"20\" /> since he will guess one of the two colors. So this case gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/e/52ea1510ab9c09ca66c1015e9d6275b0d11fe5fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{4}{7} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{2}{7}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"78\" height=\"38\" />. \nIf Nathan incorrectly guessed a blue on his first move, that means he guessed a red, so he is left with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> reds and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> blues. The most optimal move here is for Nathan to guess blue again with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/7/8f7cc5fcd0081b8887489169bc6b4e2a41a87cc4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4/6 = 2/3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"81\" height=\"20\" />. So this case gives him <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/0/540d6b810505ac4f8318efdb55c83ff150ef06ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3}{7} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{2}{7}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"78\" height=\"38\" />.\nSo the expected number of correctly guessed cards on the second guess is also <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/b/07b007731c8aa702e6c3bdbb06ec8e415ab7dc92.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4/7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"27\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Third_guess\">Third guess</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Sharma_Math_Exam_2026_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Third guess\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<ul><li>Case 1: Second guess and first guess were right* Nathan is left with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> reds and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> blues. The most optimal move is to guess red with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/a/daadf8d12321aa8913af98e9ccb98f4ddfd9616c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3/5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"26\" height=\"18\" />. This case gives him <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/8/b98e57a5451e22da60c40fdfcea706afb90fc36d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{4}{7} \\cdot \\frac{3}{6} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5} = \\frac{6}{35}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"114\" height=\"38\" />.</li></ul>\n<ul><li>Case 2: Second guess was right, first guess was wrong* Nathan is left with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> reds and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> blues. The most optimal move is to guess blue with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/a/daadf8d12321aa8913af98e9ccb98f4ddfd9616c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3/5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"26\" height=\"18\" />. This case gives him <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/9/bf9009231dba913e0d70b8685cc83bbd1d7caf84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3}{7} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5} = \\frac{6}{35}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"114\" height=\"38\" />.</li></ul>\n<ul><li>Case 3: Second guess was wrong, first guess was wrong* Nathan is left with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> red and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> blues. The most optimal move is to guess blue with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/a/e3afa4cd0db0548f54ba32179b7a9644d5d8c181.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4/5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"26\" height=\"18\" />. This case gives him <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/4/574476f6c09d2d44d002ed25dd9263de10cd41bc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3}{7} \\cdot \\frac{2}{6} \\cdot \\frac{4}{5} = \\frac{4}{35}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"114\" height=\"38\" />.</li></ul>\n<ul><li>Case 4: Second guess was wrong, first guess was right* Nathan is left with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> of one color and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> of another color. Nathan will guess the most abundant color with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/a/daadf8d12321aa8913af98e9ccb98f4ddfd9616c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3/5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"26\" height=\"18\" />. This case gives him <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/1/bf13dde5564a888f956656d57744ed56526a78bc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{4}{7} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5} = \\frac{6}{35}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"114\" height=\"38\" />.</li></ul>\n<p>So the expected number of correctly guessed cards on guess 3 is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/f/3bf5135b804b66331b7175612e880093e1b91470.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{6}{35} \\cdot 3 + \\frac{4}{35} = \\frac{22}{35}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"137\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Fourth_guess\">Fourth guess</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Sharma_Math_Exam_2026_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Fourth guess\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Consider each case of the third guess. \n</p>\n<ul><li>Case 1:* *SC1: If this guess is correct: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/2/8e278e1583977b5e29ba3820fed1a777ab5fc1c3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{4}{7} \\cdot \\frac{3}{6} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"67\" height=\"38\" /> leads to Nathan being left with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> reds and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> blues. Nathan can guess either color with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/3/1f3cc0ad966b3e637cd199607cbf0d1befc0e491.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1/2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"29\" height=\"20\" /> to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/b/2db7be13fd27bb24b535cbc105fd816313686e68.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{4}{7} \\cdot \\frac{3}{6} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{3}{35}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"140\" height=\"38\" />.</li>\n<li>SC2: If this guess is wrong: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/8/dc84626292db58bf7567259c294bff6c9af83d78.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{4}{7} \\cdot \\frac{3}{6} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"67\" height=\"38\" /> leads to Nathan being left with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> reds and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> blue. The most optimal move is to guess red with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/c/2bcddca5092a7ec886294a0561a3f69b682f12d0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3/4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"27\" height=\"18\" /> to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/1/771d2f716579e9eff239811ca248e203c27a5f17.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{4}{7} \\cdot \\frac{3}{6} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{3}{35}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"140\" height=\"38\" />.</li></ul>\n<ul><li>Case 2:* *SC1: If this guess is correct: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/1/3515b6533e9ed98fe5596abd088d3134dad64a99.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3}{7} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"67\" height=\"38\" /> leads to Nathan being left with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> reds and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> blues. Nathan can guess either color with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/3/1f3cc0ad966b3e637cd199607cbf0d1befc0e491.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1/2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"29\" height=\"20\" /> to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/a/a9ade72a607529307cdf810bd7b73c5760a871f4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3}{7} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{3}{35}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"140\" height=\"38\" />.</li>\n<li>SC2: If this guess is wrong: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/4/264b2a493f42e05c3ba28a609119251af3b52b88.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3}{7} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"67\" height=\"38\" /> leads to Nathan being left with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> blues and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> red. The most optimal move is to guess blue with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/c/2bcddca5092a7ec886294a0561a3f69b682f12d0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3/4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"27\" height=\"18\" /> to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/6/ad69d41ac8221bae8937e8e7c01137db49ad90b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3}{7} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{3}{35}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"140\" height=\"38\" />.</li></ul>\n<ul><li>Case 3:* *SC1: If this guess is correct: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/7/5378ab8eeb0cfea9a1c1b548ea58c62abd0d7398.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3}{7} \\cdot \\frac{2}{6} \\cdot \\frac{4}{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"67\" height=\"38\" /> leads to Nathan being left with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> blues and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> red. The most optimal move is to guess blue with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/c/2bcddca5092a7ec886294a0561a3f69b682f12d0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3/4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"27\" height=\"18\" /> to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/c/d9c2cec9ebb5af3c23785df4198b90921dd20652.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3}{7} \\cdot \\frac{2}{6} \\cdot \\frac{4}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{3}{35}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"140\" height=\"38\" />.</li>\n<li>SC2: If this guess is wrong: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/5/bf5aa4f638514632871107f35a56adc4963790ef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3}{7} \\cdot \\frac{2}{6} \\cdot \\frac{1}{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"67\" height=\"38\" /> leads to Nathan being left with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> blues. Nathan is guaranteed to guess every card moving forward correctly, so this gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/3/f83357098bd8e87011593c519728bcc150eeccb3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3}{7} \\cdot \\frac{2}{6} \\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\cdot 1 = \\frac{1}{35}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"136\" height=\"38\" />.</li></ul>\n<ul><li>Case 4:* *SC1: If this guess is correct: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/8/c68e9e884fb33978e8db1e3fceb7bec2859fafe7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{4}{7} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"67\" height=\"38\" /> leads to Nathan being left with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> of each color. Nathan will guess either color with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/3/1f3cc0ad966b3e637cd199607cbf0d1befc0e491.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1/2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"29\" height=\"20\" />, giving <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/a/0aa6198d0e7b08102c3e635c88b120044a7400a7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{4}{7} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{3}{35}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"140\" height=\"38\" />.</li>\n<li>SC2: If this guess is wrong: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/3/f73109948606494c97c024517e7ef87af57847e9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{4}{7} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"67\" height=\"38\" /> leads to Nathan being left with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> of one color and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> of another color. Nathan will guess the most abundant color with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/c/2bcddca5092a7ec886294a0561a3f69b682f12d0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3/4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"27\" height=\"18\" />, giving <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/0/8e0f1e5901838d0de9eb28d630116e29006c3a32.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{4}{7} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{3}{35}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"140\" height=\"38\" />.</li></ul>\n<p>So the expected number of correctly guessed cards on the fourth guess is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/3/2b374a6ab905899a5b2de51c5965a6452752ec3b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3}{35} \\cdot 7 + \\frac{1}{35} = \\frac{22}{35}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"137\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Fifth_guess\">Fifth guess</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Sharma_Math_Exam_2026_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Fifth guess\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<ul><li>Case 1: Nathan is left with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> cards of one color and one card of another color. Nathan will guess the most abundant color with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/b/38bd36e353df831303c0de895a9ec614cf3e7202.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2/3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"26\" height=\"18\" />.*</li></ul>\n<p>This can be achieved if: \n</p>\n<ul><li>Guess 3 case 4 SC1 is correct or incorrect: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/5/335309ac4b9da9fd712f15ff78f04a3150c22bfd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{4}{7} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{4}{35}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"162\" height=\"38\" /></li>\n<li>Guess 3 case 4 SC2 is correct: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/6/f6664f2f59755fa2dbfaefbf11d68fd2349309e7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{4}{7} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{4} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{2}{35}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"166\" height=\"38\" /></li>\n<li>Guess 3 case 3 SC1 is correct: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/5/b859a48a5c55636ede7793f2e9ca74fb68c8419e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3}{7} \\cdot \\frac{2}{6} \\cdot \\frac{4}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{4} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{2}{35}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"166\" height=\"38\" /></li>\n<li>Guess 3 case 2 SC2 is correct: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/9/e89314808ba68f5940c522183a1c87c094b283c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3}{7} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{4} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{2}{35}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"166\" height=\"38\" /></li>\n<li>Guess 3 case 2 SC1 is correct or incorrect: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/9/0e9004134f08a544a3b53a389d84c286ceb6001e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3}{7} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{4}{35}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"162\" height=\"38\" /></li>\n<li>Guess 3 case 1 SC2 is correct: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/e/4be5453bc95fbb4a1062e516a72fe55424308e81.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{4}{7} \\cdot \\frac{3}{6} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{4} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{2}{35}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"166\" height=\"38\" /></li>\n<li>Guess 3 case 1 SC1 is correct or incorrect: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/c/8cced155706422b5e53e22e5721bbbd14eaf9e16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{4}{7} \\cdot \\frac{3}{6} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{4}{35}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"162\" height=\"38\" /></li></ul>\n<p>Sum <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/b/ccb81b23d96355993b29181e7c995eb0776af358.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$= \\frac{12}{35} + \\frac{8}{35} = \\frac{20}{35}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"134\" height=\"38\" />\n</p>\n<ul><li>Case 2: Nathan is left with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> cards of one color. Nathan is guaranteed to guess the other card correctly with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.*</li></ul>\n<p>This can be achieved if: \n</p>\n<ul><li>Guess 3 case 4 SC2 is incorrect: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/e/dae9f20d541fb92e557f08c6d56108a110979355.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{4}{7} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 1 = \\frac{1}{35}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"162\" height=\"38\" /></li>\n<li>Guess 3 case 3 SC2 is correct or incorrect: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/d/63d81534f829f61d6cb460b369c1c282c752ac31.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3}{7} \\cdot \\frac{2}{6} \\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 = \\frac{1}{35}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"158\" height=\"38\" /></li>\n<li>Guess 3 case 3 SC1 is incorrect: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/2/ba2dca0b028d09d1a5b16cae016d87ceced4a8e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3}{7} \\cdot \\frac{2}{6} \\cdot \\frac{4}{5} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 1 = \\frac{1}{35}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"162\" height=\"38\" /></li>\n<li>Guess 3 case 2 SC2 is incorrect: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/7/e975ff02f9957e02474c08e93cce022be58cc3e4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3}{7} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 1 = \\frac{1}{35}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"162\" height=\"38\" /></li>\n<li>Guess 3 case 1 SC2 is incorrect: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/1/91194cb1a0c499d119c83781088526ba71f7ef7f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{4}{7} \\cdot \\frac{3}{6} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 1 = \\frac{1}{35}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"162\" height=\"38\" /></li></ul>\n<p>Sum <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/3/b9303a7f5ea2730a3dbe46f0b357e2c1c954ba33.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$= \\frac{5}{35}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"43\" height=\"38\" />\nSo the expected number of correctly guessed cards on the fifth guess is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/4/df4390598d32f3112253941f84289d65b334eb20.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{20}{35} + \\frac{5}{35} = \\frac{25}{35} = \\frac{5}{7}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"152\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Sixth_guess\">Sixth guess</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Sharma_Math_Exam_2026_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Sixth guess\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<ul><li>Case 1: Nathan is left with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> cards of the same color* This can be achieved if fifth guess case 1 is wrong or if Nathan guesses any card in case 2. Notice how in fifth guess case 1, everything is being multiplied with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/b/38bd36e353df831303c0de895a9ec614cf3e7202.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2/3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"26\" height=\"18\" /> for the expected value, but to count the probability of it being wrong, we need to multiply everything with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/7/217aedbdc339bacc8ba075a2ec16902b098194e3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1/3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"29\" height=\"20\" /> instead. To do so, Nathan can sneakily multiply the sum by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/3/1f3cc0ad966b3e637cd199607cbf0d1befc0e491.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1/2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"29\" height=\"20\" /> since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/3/3d3df477ecdae67c3ceab14b5a6fa3ac928a8572.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2}{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"78\" height=\"38\" />. So the probability that fifth guess case 1 is wrong is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/3/293fb7ec887db394192d34b919e51e167a087e37.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{20}{35} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{10}{35}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"97\" height=\"38\" />. This is added to the probability of Nathan guessing any card in case 2 to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/a/f6ac60f7cc15a36501493d581e0a5d83d4869e81.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{10}{35} + \\frac{5}{35} = \\frac{15}{35}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"115\" height=\"38\" />.</li></ul>\n<ul><li>Case 2: Nathan is left with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> red and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> blue card* This can be achieved if fifth guess case 1 is correct. After that, Nathan can guess any color with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/3/1f3cc0ad966b3e637cd199607cbf0d1befc0e491.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1/2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"29\" height=\"20\" />. This gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/3/293fb7ec887db394192d34b919e51e167a087e37.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{20}{35} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{10}{35}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"97\" height=\"38\" />.</li></ul>\n<p>So the expected number of correctly guessed cards on the sixth guess is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/9/d597a94f62739d01c338843f29e9fdfd22def1cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{15}{35} + \\frac{10}{35} = \\frac{25}{35} = \\frac{5}{7}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"152\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>On the seventh guess, Nathan is guaranteed to guess the card (expected value <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />). \n</p><p>Therefore, the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c33534b08bab0077872cc0ba148d19f856a098e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{4}{7} + \\frac{4}{7} + \\frac{22}{35} + \\frac{22}{35} + \\frac{5}{7} + \\frac{5}{7} + 1 = \\frac{169}{35}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"296\" height=\"38\" />. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/2/ea2ebdddbe25fd8bec9f9d5aee7302d9ccc8a54e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$169 + 35 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(A) } 204}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"168\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017111932\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.043 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.078 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 716/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 29228/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:36069:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017111932 and revision id 279837. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}